-- HIC.1: add evidence + responsible to oe_mapping.
-- Replaces oe_mapping only; every other column of the approved row is untouched.
update public.shco_policy_masters
   set oe_mapping = '[{"oe_code": "HIC.1.a", "requirement": "The infection prevention and control programme is documented and aims at preventing and reducing healthcare associated infection risk", "steps": "Steps 1, 4-11 and 26", "evidence": "The written annual infection prevention and control programme approved by the IPCC and endorsed by the management of {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} before the period it covers begins, stating the objectives expressed so that meeting them can be demonstrated, the surveillance to be conducted and in which areas, the audits planned with their frequency and who performs them, the training planned by staff group, the improvement projects with the person accountable for each, the resources required, and how and to whom progress will be reported; the numeric targets adopted for the period with the baseline of {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} that each was set against; the documented annual infection risk assessment preceding the programme, recording the services actually provided and the invasive procedures and devices each involves, the patient population served including any concentration of immunocompromised, paediatric, obstetric or elderly patients, the hospital''s own surveillance data for the preceding period including any cluster, local and regional antimicrobial resistance patterns drawn from its own culture reports, the physical plant covering ventilation, water systems, layout and ongoing construction, the findings of the previous assessment tool review, audits and any accreditation or statutory inspection, and communicable disease activity in the surrounding community, with the risks ranked so that effort follows the largest risk rather than the most visible one; the IPCC minute tabling that risk assessment and the record of its retention; IPCC minutes recording the review of progress against the programme at each meeting, and the end-of-period closing report stating in writing what was and was not achieved; the infection control manual carrying the full contents listed at step 4, with the single controlled master copy held by the Infection Control Team and the point-of-care copy held in each clinical area, together with the arrangement that keeps an electronic manual readable when the network or a device is unavailable; each document in the manual carrying a version number, an approval date and a scheduled review date on its face, with an amendment history showing what changed, why and on whose approval; the IPCC approval of every revision recorded before it took effect, the withdrawal record for each superseded version removed from every point of use, and the single dated copy retained by the Infection Control Team; the record of any policy reviewed ahead of its scheduled date under one of the four triggers at step 5, with the trigger stated — a changed national guideline, statutory rule or NABH requirement, an outbreak, incident or adverse event exposing a gap, an audit or assessment finding the policy impractical as written, or a new service, procedure or equipment it does not cover; surveillance records for surgical site infection, catheter-associated urinary tract infection, central line-associated bloodstream infection and ventilator-associated events so far as each applies to the services {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} provides, naming who collected the data, with the IPCC minute of their review at every meeting and the dated record that the results were fed back to the clinical area they came from; environmental surveillance results — microbiological and physical monitoring of operating theatres and other critical areas covering air sampling, ventilation and filter performance, temperature and humidity, with any surface or water sampling undertaken — evidenced as having reached the IPCC rather than remaining in an engineering file; a recorded corrective action carrying a named owner and a re-test for every out-of-limit environmental result; for each improvement project in the annual programme, the record identifying how it addresses all five elements of the combined approach at step 10 — system change, training and education, monitoring and feedback, reminders and communication, and culture; training records by name, date, topic and trainer covering clinical, nursing, housekeeping, laundry, kitchen, waste-handling, security and administrative staff at a depth appropriate to each role, showing induction training completed before the new joiner began independent work, the recurring interval thereafter, training triggered by a material change of policy, technique or equipment, and training delivered in response to an audit finding, outbreak or incident; for every taught technique — hand hygiene, PPE removal, spill management — the training record noting that competence was observed and not merely that the session was attended; outbreak records under step 26 showing the cases confirmed against the surveillance definitions with when and where they occurred, the time control measures began relative to the time suspicion arose, the notification to the Infection Control Officer and the head of the affected area, the convening of the IPCC or its emergency subgroup, the investigation of source and route with laboratory involvement and the review of practice, equipment, environment and staffing in the affected area, the sequence of events, the measures taken and the outcome; the outbreak conclusion reported to the IPCC with the changes required to prevent recurrence, and evidence that the lessons reached both the revision of the relevant policy and the next annual risk assessment", "responsible": "Infection Control Team drafts the annual risk assessment, the written annual programme and the infection control manual, and keeps every document in the manual current and version-controlled; Infection Control Officer accountable for the technical content of the programme, for the surveillance and environmental monitoring results reaching the IPCC, and for leading outbreak investigation and instituting immediate control measures under step 26; Infection Control Nurse delivers and records the training, maintains the programme records and prepares the data and analysis the IPCC needs; IPCC approves the risk assessment, the annual programme, the manual and every revision to it, reviews progress at each meeting and closes the period out in writing; management of {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} endorses the annual programme before the period it covers begins and receives the outbreak conclusion; heads of department own the improvement projects assigned to them and remain the owners of infection control practice within their own areas, which the Infection Control Team advises and audits without relieving them of that responsibility"}, {"oe_code": "HIC.1.b", "requirement": "The programme is reviewed using an infection control assessment tool", "steps": "Steps 12-13", "evidence": "The written record of which infection control assessment tool {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} has adopted and the interval at which the review is conducted; the completed assessment held in full for each cycle, distinguishable from the routine practice audits at step 3 in that it assesses whether the programme itself is sound rather than whether staff followed a policy; where the World Health Organization''s Infection Prevention and Control Assessment Framework is the tool adopted, the scored assessment across its eight components — the programme itself, written guidelines, education and training, surveillance of healthcare associated infection, multimodal implementation strategies, monitoring, audit and feedback, workload, staffing and bed occupancy, and the built environment, materials and equipment — with each component scored out of 100, the total out of 800, and the resulting level recorded as inadequate at 0 to 200, basic at 201 to 400, intermediate at 401 to 600 or advanced at 601 to 800; the name and role of the Infection Control Officer or Infection Control Nurse who led the review; the supporting evidence relied on for each scored item, producible on request, with the record that any item which could not be evidenced was scored as not met rather than credited from recollection; the written post-review summary setting out the score obtained overall and by component, each gap identified, the action proposed for that gap, the person accountable for it and the date it is due; the minute of the next IPCC meeting tabling that summary, with the discussion recorded; the standing-item record showing progress against the previous cycle''s actions carried at every IPCC meeting until each action was closed; the retained scores from successive cycles held together and compared, so that the direction of travel across cycles is visible rather than a single score describing one moment; the recorded investigation and stated reason for any component score or total that has fallen since the previous cycle, rather than an explanation offered without enquiry; the escalation record under step 14 for any finding requiring money or posts the committee could not authorise, together with management''s written decision on it", "responsible": "Infection Control Officer or Infection Control Nurse of {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} leads the review, scores it honestly against producible evidence and prepares the post-review summary; Infection Control Team assembles and retains the supporting evidence behind each scored item and holds the successive scores for comparison; IPCC receives the summary, agrees the action against each gap with a named owner and a due date, and carries progress as a standing item until every action is closed; heads of department own the gap-closure actions assigned to their areas; management of {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} decides on findings escalated under step 14 because they require resources beyond the committee''s authority, and records the reason where a request is not met"}, {"oe_code": "HIC.1.c", "requirement": "A multidisciplinary infection control committee and an infection control team coordinate all IPC activities", "steps": "Steps 2-3", "evidence": "The terms of reference of the Infection Prevention and Control Committee carrying its member list by name and designation, revised whenever a member changes; evidence in that member list of multidisciplinary composition, with representation drawn from medical and surgical clinical services, nursing, microbiology or laboratory services, pharmacy, housekeeping and environmental services, central sterile supply and operating theatre services, engineering and maintenance, and hospital administration or management; the record of who chairs the committee, the meeting frequency adopted by {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} and the quorum defined for a valid meeting; for every meeting, the circulated agenda, the signed attendance record evidencing that the quorum was met, and minutes recording each decision, the person made responsible for each action and the date by which it is due; evidence that the minutes of the previous meeting were tabled at the next one, so that open actions are visibly carried forward rather than lost; the minutes filed chronologically and retained for the period set at step 28; the appointment record of the Infection Control Officer with the qualification relied on, being a medically qualified person who is a microbiologist or a physician with infection control training; the appointment record of at least one Infection Control Nurse, stating the time committed to the role and evidencing that the time is protected rather than absorbed into a full clinical roster; daily round records of clinical areas showing practice observed and breaches identified at the point they occurred; records of surveillance data collected and collated by the team; investigation records for suspected outbreaks and for any cluster of infection; records of staff training delivered by the team; audit reports on compliance with each infection control policy, with the dated record that the result was fed back to the area audited; records of advice given to clinical teams on isolation decisions and patient placement; the data and analysis prepared by the team for each IPCC meeting; the display in every clinical area of {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} of the current team members'' names and contact details, positioned so that any member of staff can reach them without going through a supervisor", "responsible": "Management of {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} constitutes the multidisciplinary committee, appoints its chair and the Infection Control Officer, and protects the time committed to the Infection Control Nurse role; the committee chair accountable for the circulated agenda, the quorum and the minuting of decisions, owners and due dates; Infection Control Officer leads the Infection Control Team and supplies the clinical and microbiological judgement the programme depends on; Infection Control Nurse conducts the daily rounds, surveillance, audit and training, maintains the team''s records and is the first point of contact for staff on infection control questions; each committee member accountable for representing their discipline and for closing the actions minuted against them; heads of department remain the owners of infection control practice in their own areas, which the team advises and audits without assuming that responsibility"}, {"oe_code": "HIC.1.d", "requirement": "IEC programme for the community, and participation in managing community outbreaks and pandemics", "steps": "Steps 23-25", "evidence": "The information, education and communication calendar for the year, identifying for each activity the topic, the audience, the format, the person responsible and the date; coverage in that calendar of hand hygiene, respiratory hygiene and cough etiquette, safe handling and disposal of waste at home, food and water hygiene, care of wounds and devices after discharge, appropriate use of antibiotics, and the locally relevant communicable diseases; the printed material and posters themselves, in the languages that patients of {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} actually read and using pictures where literacy cannot be assumed, with evidence of display in outpatient and emergency waiting areas, wards and reception; records of verbal counselling of patients and attendants at admission and before discharge, particularly where care will continue at home; outreach records for health camps, school sessions and community meetings held at the frequency adopted by {{HOSPITAL_NAME}}; the activity record for each item carrying the date, topic, audience and approximate number reached; the IPCC minute evidencing that the IEC record was reviewed and the programme adjusted rather than merely repeated; the written outbreak and pandemic response plan stating what triggers its activation and who has the authority to activate it, the internal command structure during activation by role, how surge capacity in beds, isolation space, staff, oxygen, PPE and consumables would be created and from where, how screening and segregation of arriving patients would be organised at the entrance, how staff would be protected, monitored and relieved including the arrangements for exposed or infected staff, how the hospital communicates with the district health authority, with its own staff and with patients and the public, and how essential non-outbreak services would continue; the named point of contact of {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} with the district or municipal health authority; records of the drill or tabletop exercise testing the plan at the stated interval, with participation recorded, the findings noted and the resulting revision to the plan; where a public health emergency has been declared, the directions received from the competent health authority, the data reported in the format and at the frequency specified, and the record of staff, facilities or information made available within capacity; the current notifiable disease list for the State kept available where clinicians can see it; the record naming the person responsible for making notifications and the named deputy; a retained copy of every notification made with its date and recipient, evidencing notification within the timeframe prescribed for the disease concerned and immediate notification of any suspected outbreak or unusual cluster without waiting for laboratory confirmation where clinical suspicion alone requires reporting; records of cooperation with any investigation undertaken by the health authority, including the records and access provided; the record that any cluster of infection among patients or staff of {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} was reported internally to the Infection Control Officer on the same day it was suspected, and externally where the disease or the scale required it", "responsible": "Infection Control Nurse plans and delivers the IEC calendar and keeps the activity records for IPCC review; Infection Control Officer accountable for the technical content of patient and community material, for the clinical content of the outbreak and pandemic response plan, and for receiving same-day internal reports of any suspected cluster; the named notification officer of {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} and the named deputy make every statutory notification within the prescribed timeframe and retain a copy of each; head of the institution owns the outbreak and pandemic response plan, holds the relationship with the district or municipal health authority through the named point of contact, and authorises the release of staff, facilities or information during a declared emergency; treating clinicians identify notifiable disease at the point of care and report it without waiting for laboratory confirmation where suspicion alone requires it; IPCC reviews the IEC record and the drill findings and requires the programme and the plan to be revised accordingly"}, {"oe_code": "HIC.1.e", "requirement": "Management makes the resources required for the programme available", "steps": "Steps 14-17", "evidence": "The written annual statement of what the programme needs, derived from the annual programme at step 7 and covering personal protective equipment, hand hygiene consumables, disinfectants and cleaning agents, sterilisation supplies and monitoring indicators, biomedical waste consumables, isolation facility equipment, surveillance and audit tools, staff immunisation, training materials, and any staffing or infrastructure requirement identified by the assessment tool review; the record of its submission to management and management''s decision recorded in writing, including, where a request was not met in full, the reason and the alternative control to be applied in the interim; both the request and the decision retained, with the outcome recorded in the IPCC minutes; the entry in the risk register of {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} for every unfunded infection control requirement, with evidence that it was reviewed at each IPCC meeting until resolved; the stock register for infection control consumables; the minimum stock level and reorder level defined for each item on the basis of the hospital''s own consumption, with replenishment records evidencing that reorder was triggered at the reorder level rather than at exhaustion; expiry-date check records for disinfectants, handrub and sterilisation indicators, with the record of expired stock removed from use; the record of every stock-out stating the clinical areas affected, the interim measure applied and the corrective action, together with its report to the IPCC; the Infection Control Officer''s recorded agreement to any substitution of a specified product with an alternative during a shortage, evidencing that an ineffective or incompatible agent was not introduced under supply pressure; the written infection control staffing establishment stating the number of posts and the whole-time equivalent hours committed, the basis on which that number was arrived at, and its position against the benchmark of at least one full-time equivalent trained infection prevention professional per 250 beds treated as a floor rather than a target; formal training certificates or training records retained for every person appointed to an infection control role; where the benchmark is not met, the shortfall recorded as a risk under step 14 together with the interim arrangement being relied on; Infection Control Team round records verifying the physical means of infection control at the point of care — hand hygiene facilities with running water, soap and alcohol-based handrub with dispensers filled and functional, personal protective equipment available in the appropriate sizes and quantities in each clinical area, puncture-resistant sharps containers within arm''s reach wherever sharps are used and not overfilled, colour-coded biomedical waste containers with liners at every point of waste generation, spill management materials accessible in every clinical area, the isolation facilities described at steps 18 to 22, and functioning ventilation in critical areas; the record of every deficiency found on those rounds together with its correction, and, where a deficiency could not be corrected on the spot, the log entry with a target date and the evidence that it was tracked to closure at the IPCC", "responsible": "Management of {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} is accountable for making the resources available through a documented process rather than case by case, for recording its decision in writing on every request, and for stating the reason and the interim control where a request is not met in full; Infection Control Team prepares the annual resource statement from the programme and verifies point-of-care infrastructure on its rounds, recording each deficiency and its correction; Infection Control Officer agrees any product substitution during a shortage and is accountable for the staffing establishment being stated and any shortfall recorded as a risk; the administrative or purchase function maintains the stock register, the minimum and reorder levels and the continuity of supply, and reports every stock-out to the IPCC; engineering maintains the ventilation, water and physical conditions the programme depends on; heads of department close the deficiencies logged in their own areas by the target date; IPCC records the outcome of each resource request in its minutes and reviews every unfunded requirement on the risk register until it is resolved"}, {"oe_code": "HIC.1.f", "requirement": "Isolation and barrier nursing facilities are available", "steps": "Steps 18-22", "evidence": "The record of which rooms {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} has designated for isolation and how many, evidencing that isolation capacity is planned in advance rather than improvised during an outbreak; for each designated room, evidence that it is a single-occupancy room with its own door that can be kept closed, has its own toilet and washing facility wherever the layout permits, has hand hygiene facilities at the entrance and inside, holds a supply of personal protective equipment immediately outside or in an anteroom so that staff can put it on before entering, holds patient-care equipment dedicated to that room — stethoscope, thermometer, blood pressure cuff — which is not shared with other patients and is decontaminated before leaving the room, has a clear means of displaying the precaution in force at the entrance without naming the patient''s diagnosis where that would breach confidentiality, and has a waste and linen disposal arrangement that does not require carrying uncontained material through clean areas; the isolation equipment inventory with the record of its check at the stated interval, evidencing that the room is ready before it is needed rather than equipped at the moment of admission; where airborne infection isolation is provided, the room''s engineering specification and the recorded verification at the stated interval of a pressure differential of at least 2.5 pascals, equivalent to 0.01 inches of water gauge, with at least 12 air changes per hour for a newly built or renovated room and not fewer than 6 air changes per hour where an existing room is used, together with the arrangement for exhausting room air directly to the outside away from air intakes and occupied areas or passing it through a high-efficiency particulate air filter where it must be recirculated; the record that the door is kept closed for the negative pressure to hold, and the fit-test records for the particulate respirators worn by staff entering; where {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} has no airborne infection isolation room, the written statement of the interim measure applied — a single room with the door closed, exhaust ventilation to the outside, masking of the patient — and the referral arrangement under which the patient is transferred to a facility that has one, recorded as a resource gap under step 14; records of Infection Control Team consultation before any patient requiring isolation was placed other than in a single room, with the decision and its reasoning recorded; placement records evidencing either cohorting of patients infected or colonised with the same organism in one bay or area with no other patients admitted to it and, where practicable, staff assigned to that cohort, or the selection of a lowest-risk roommate avoiding patients who are immunocompromised, who have an open wound or an invasive device, or whose expected stay is long; the record of the spatial separation of at least one metre, approximately three feet, and the drawn curtain maintained where a patient requiring droplet precautions had to remain in a multi-bed room; the explicitly recorded emergency measure and the transfer arrangement for any patient requiring airborne precautions placed in an open bay, evidencing that such patients are not cohorted with patients under other precautions; barrier nursing audit records evidencing that the precaution in force is displayed at the entrance and complied with by every person entering including doctors, visitors, students and support staff, that personal protective equipment appropriate to the precaution is put on before entry and removed before leaving with hand hygiene performed after removal, that the number of staff and visitors entering is kept to the minimum necessary and visitors are instructed before their first entry, that equipment dedicated to the room stays in the room and is decontaminated before removal, that linen and waste are contained within the room before being removed, and that movement of the patient out of the room is limited to what is clinically necessary with the receiving area informed in advance and the patient masked or the affected site covered during transfer; the enhanced and terminal cleaning records for the isolation room under the environmental cleaning policy; the isolation register recording for every patient placed in isolation or barrier nursing the patient identifier, ward and room, the date isolation began and the date it ended, the indication as the organism or syndrome with the route of transmission, the category of precaution applied, whether a single room was used and, if not, what alternative placement was used and why, and the reason for discontinuation; the IPCC minute of its review of that register for what it reveals about the hospital rather than about individual patients — whether isolation capacity was sufficient, whether patients waited for placement, whether precautions were started promptly after suspicion arose, and whether any avoidable transmission followed a delay; barrier nursing drill records at the stated interval, with the observations made and any corrective action taken recorded", "responsible": "Head of the institution provides and funds the designated isolation rooms and the airborne infection isolation capacity, and authorises the referral arrangement where {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} has none; engineering maintains and verifies the pressure differential, the air change rate and the exhaust arrangement at the stated interval and records each verification; Infection Control Officer authorises isolation, decides the category of precaution in force, and is consulted before any patient requiring isolation is placed other than in a single room; Infection Control Nurse maintains the isolation register and the isolation equipment inventory, audits barrier nursing practice and runs the drills; nursing in-charge of each area accountable for the precaution being displayed at the entrance, for barrier nursing practice at the bedside, and for instructing visitors before their first entry; housekeeping for the enhanced and terminal cleaning of the room under the environmental cleaning policy; treating clinicians for limiting movement of the patient out of the room to what is clinically necessary and for informing the receiving area in advance; IPCC reviews the register for capacity, delay and avoidable transmission and escalates any capacity shortfall as a resource gap under step 14"}]'::jsonb
 where standard_code = 'HIC.1';
